and a debate-free fall leave the margin uncertain"], "rationale": "Haaland's overwhelming money edge, her profile as a former Interior Secretary, and New Mexico's Democratic lean give her a clear advantage, and raters and betting markets alike treat her as the strong favorite. The main uncertainty is the size of the win, given limited and somewhat divergent polling rather than any sign of a genuine contest.", "watch": "A late external shock, a consolidated Republican turnout surge in a low-turnout open-seat election, or new polling showing Hull gaining could narrow the race.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Michelle Lujan Grisham (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "New Mexico's open governor's race pits Democrat Deb Haaland, a former congresswoman and Interior Secretary, against Rio Rancho mayor Gregg Hull. The state has leaned Democratic in recent statewide contests, and Haaland enters the fall with overwhelming resource advantages: she has raised about $11.1 million to Hull's roughly $474,000 and holds a large cash edge for the final stretch.\n\nIndependent ratings uniformly favor Democrats, with several calling the race solid or safe and others likely Democratic. The limited polling available, conducted in July and August, showed Haaland ahead by margins ranging from one to seventeen points, with the most recent and best-regarded survey giving her a six-point lead. Betting markets put her chances above ninety percent. Hull needs to consolidate Republicans and cut into Haaland's standing with swing voters, something the evidence so far does not show happening.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9664, "margin": 10.58}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9571, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9605, "margin": 10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.948, "margin": 10.2}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9415, "margin": 9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9502, "margin": 10.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.95